WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.440
This personal watercraft leaps, dives, and
barrel rolls with the waves, but it doesn't

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:16.380
capsize. It's been engineered to return to
an upright position in the water, and when it

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.000
comes to the fun factor, this diving
watercraft leaves other recreational boats in

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:30.190
its wake. To make the diving Watercrafts
passenger cabin, a worker sprays a gel coat

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.800
onto a form and covers it with fiberglass.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:38.770
A computer driven router then carves into
marine grade plywood to produce structural

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.870
backing for the sides of the cockpit.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:48.790
A worker slathers adhesive putty onto the
plywood part and applies more adhesive to the

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.170
fiberglass cockpit.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.530
He presses the plywood to the glued areas of
the cockpit and brushes resin onto the front

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:06.890
of the plywood. After cutting the cockpit
section in two, the crew glues the parts to

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.750
the inside of the fuselage.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.720
The fabricating team applies adhesive putty
to the cone shaped bumper known as the

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.130
sacrificial nose.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.820
They slide it onto the front of the fuselage
and then screw the bumper in place

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.340
temporarily while the adhesive sets.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.220
Another worker stands all the fiberglass
parts of the watercraft to remove bumps and

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.670
blemishes. This piece is the engine hatch.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.520
The team bolts the aluminum scaffold to the
floor of the fuselage.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.810
This is a test fit to confirm that the
dimensions are exactly right.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.780
As they install the scaffold, they use dummy
wing shafts to correctly position it within

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.390
the vessel. They confirm that the dimensions
are right, and that the scaffold sits plumb

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.130
to the base of the watercraft.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.110
The next team inserts the hatch frame in the
rim around the cockpit.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.360
They'll bond the frame in place later with a
liquid plastic and adhesive putty.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.530
After priming the sanded surface of the
watercraft, team members lowered the 300

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.510
horsepower engine into the hatch.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.590
This is a dry fit to confirm everything
lines up correctly.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.640
They'll eventually remove the engine for the
final paint and artwork.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:38.980
As the dry fit continues, they connect the
air tubes to the snorkel fins that deliver

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.150
air to the engine.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.910
The ancient hatch has by now also been
primed, and they fit it to the diving

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.470
watercrafts main body.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.600
The crew verifies that the hatch sits flush
with the rest of the fuselage.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.200
A worker installs the exhaust outlet into
the hull at the rear of the watercraft.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.360
Crew members slide elevator fins into
bushings at the back.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.990
These fins will control the pitch axis of
the watercraft.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.530
Next they install the jet propulsion system.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.910
Back to the cockpit.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.950
The crew bolts the control pedals to the
floor and run cables to the jet propulsion

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.020
unit. The elevator fins and the steering
system.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.760
They reinstall the defense scaffold, which
has been powder coated in a yellow color.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.560
The crew connects the defense to the
scaffold.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.120
They situate the control panel just above
the control pedals.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:54.150
They run a metal linkage from the dive fins
to a control stick.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.300
They then test the control stick and confirm
it moves the dive planes easily and

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:11.250
correctly. Bucket seats made of lightweight
carbon fiber minimize the load for added

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:18.640
speed. They took a rubber gasket into the
framework for the clear canopy that will

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.110
surround the occupants.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:25.470
They add a bead of sealant where the gasket
mates to the window frame.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:31.450
The vessel canopy is made of thick, aircraft
grade acrylic.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.090
It's been tinted to repel the sun's rays and
keep the temperature cooler in the cockpit.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.330
The bat canopy serves as a hatch for
accessing the cockpit.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:49.020
Finally, an artist does a custom paint job
and makes the fuselage look fierce.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.720
With the mechanical components reinstalled,
this diving watercraft is ready to hit the

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.380
water. Fasten your seatbelt.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.120
It should be quite a ride.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:14.090
A diesel exhaust fluid, or Def tank heater
is a device which prevents diesel exhaust

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.440
fluid from freezing.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.300
Diesel exhaust fluid is a solution sprayed
into the exhaust system of a vehicle with a

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.560
diesel engine to break down dangerous,
polluting chemicals the tailpipe would

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.700
otherwise emit.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.070
A worker programs a robotic bender to bend
the cut tube to the required shape.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.300
These bends are so complex that no
traditional manually operated bending machine

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.610
would be able to do them this accurately.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.070
The heater sits right inside the holding
tank.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.740
That's why manufacturers use stainless steel
rather than ordinary steel for this tubing.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.720
Stainless steel better resist the corrosive
ness of the fluid.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:19.320
At the next station, a worker inserts the
bent tube into the tank heater sensor unit.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.570
The tube fits through openings in the sensor
unit's header.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:31.230
When connected by a cable to the vehicle's
computer, the sensor unit detects the level,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.630
temperature, and quality of the Def.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:41.880
Next, the worker puts the tubing in a punch
press, which shapes the ends.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.490
He puts those shaped ends in a bender which
curves them.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:00.770
Another tube made the same way siphons from
the holding tank to a device that sprays the

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.390
fluid on the exhaust stream.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:07.820
He installs a rubber O ring onto this siphon
tube and applies lubricant.

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:13.700
He installs the tube into the header, and
the O ring fits into a notch.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.850
The worker then installs a bracket at the
base of the heater, riveting it to the

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:29.320
tubing. The bracket holds a rubber grommet
that secures the heater in place

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.150
inside the tank.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:37.690
Further up, he rivets on a second bracket to
stabilize the sensor tube that runs down the

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.190
center of the sensor unit.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.630
This prevents the tube from rattling.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:50.520
Then he installs the return tube, which
feeds any excess unsprayed def back to the

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:57.150
holding tank. Retaining brackets apply
pressure to all the O rings, sealing the tube

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.800
connections against leaks.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.710
He flips the heater over to attach the drain
tube, which is the bottom half of the return

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:17.680
tube. He caps all the tubes to keep them
clean until

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:19.690
the heater is installed in the vehicle.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.020
He attaches a nylon filter to the siphon
tube, securing it in place with the retaining

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:38.220
clip. As the siphon tube draws the Def from
the holding tank toward the sprayer, the

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.920
filter removes contaminants such as sand.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.400
Finally, this rubber grommet snaps into the
bracket at the base of the heater.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:52.530
The grommet fits over a raised bump on the
tank floor, holding the unit securely in

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:58.860
place. Once connected to the vehicle's
computer, the heater keeps the diesel exhaust

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:01.530
fluid from freezing, no matter how cold the
weather.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.280
Looking to get high on a new water sport
while you literally can riding a flying water

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:14.370
bike. It attaches with a long hose to the
back of a jet ski, elevating you up to almost

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:18.780
40ft above the water where you can perform
aerial flips and spins.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:24.240
The Jet Skis water thrust feeds three jet
nozzles on the flying water bike.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:30.180
One propels the bike upward, the other two
feed jet control nozzles that maneuver the

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:35.340
bike. After masking the ends of the
splitter, a worker sprays the completed

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:39.750
frame, jet control nozzles, and other
aluminum parts with powder coat.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.450
This helps protect against corrosion.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:47.920
The parts go into an oven for up to a half
hour to bake.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.110
The powder coat to a shiny finish.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:56.740
Then workers install an aluminum cam lock to
securely fasten the fire hose to the

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:02.170
coupling. Another worker attaches the
remaining components to the frame.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:06.310
First, he screws in the pair of foot plates
on which the rider stands.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:11.380
Then he bolts the bottom half of each plate
to the parallel support bars, and the top

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.510
half to just ahead of the cam lock.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.780
He mounts a padded knee rest to the support
bars.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.700
It can be easily repositioned to accommodate
the rider's height.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:33.550
On each unpainted end of the splitter, he
installs an internal support ring, then a

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:39.400
precision fit nylon bushing and an external
support ring, which he screws to the

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:49.110
splitter. The worker slides the jet control
nozzle housing over the

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.350
bushing and screws it to the internal
support ring.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:56.820
The two control nozzles maneuver the water
bike to ascend.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:01.500
You pull back gradually on both of them
simultaneously to descend.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.420
You push forward on both to spin.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.840
You move one forward and one back.

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:09.060
And to do a backflip.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.760
You yank both control nozzles all the way
back.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:16.370
He assembles the water bike's body.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:21.560
It's made of molded polyethylene with a
waterproof urethane pad to cushion the front

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.820
of the rider's body.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:24.860
The seat is made of the same padded
material.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:29.480
Both pad and seat attach with plastic clips.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:37.040
The hollow body is completely watertight,
which is why the bike floats.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.650
The final step is to mate the body to the
frame.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:46.360
The completed water bicycle weighs £30.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:54.370
Once connected to a jet ski with a fire
hose, this water bike flies at a speed of up

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:56.080
to 20mph.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:03.130
By maneuvering the jet nozzles, it does
backflips, multiple spins, and even dives

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:04.720
20ft under water.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:12.350
Solar streetlights take energy from the sun
during the day, store it, and then use it to

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:13.820
shine brightly at night.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:17.330
So even when the sun sets, they still
provide light.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:22.100
Manufacturing begins with the assembly of an
LED circuit board.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:28.130
The technician places the optics or lenses
onto the aluminum board and adds a silicone

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.260
foam gasket for waterproofing.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:34.190
She secures the assembly with the stainless
steel plate.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.990
This completes the solar streetlights.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:40.160
Led board.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:46.960
The next worker assembles the LED board to
the base of an aluminum heat sink.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.680
It will disperse excess heat from the LED
board.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:57.570
He uses several screws to secure the LED
board to the heat sink.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:03.030
He installs the driver, which will provide
power to the LED board.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:09.810
Once in place, he connects the LED driver to
the input power lead.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:14.340
While she now builds the decorative housing.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.620
He screws aluminum tiers together.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.840
He applies adhesive to the upper inner rim.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:30.810
He inserts an optically clear polycarbonate
lens and the flange adheres to the adhesive

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:38.910
coated rim. He screws the LED heatsink
assembly to an aluminum lid.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:46.390
He now applies a special adhesive to the
outside rim of the light fixture housing.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.550
He presses foam tubing onto the area.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:57.930
Once compressed by the lid, it will provide
a watertight seal with everything in an

00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.430
upside down position.

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:04.410
He lowers the assembly over the LED heatsink
and screws it to the lid.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.680
A technician plugs the fixture into an
electrical testing unit.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:16.290
He confirms that all the LEDs are
illuminating and that there are no electrical

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:25.880
issues. The manufacturers then transfer the
light fixture to a solar light factory.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:31.340
Upon arrival here, a technician does more
testing to verify that its function hasn't

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.290
been compromised along the way.

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:39.230
The team is now ready to add the solar power
system to the LED streetlight.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:44.240
A technician programs the solar charge and
load controller.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.520
Another team member places the battery that
will hold the solar charge into a vented

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:54.110
cabinet. He connects the battery to a side
clip and ground wire.

00:14:54.410 --> 00:15:00.140
He tucks insulating foam into the sides and
connects the load fuse to the battery wiring.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:04.430
He plugs the controller into the battery.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:09.560
In addition to regulating the battery charge
and energy load, it's also a timer for the

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:10.880
light's operation.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:13.760
Next up is the solar array.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:17.150
It's a group of photovoltaic panels or solar
cells.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:22.280
The technician sets the solar array front,
side down on the workbench.

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:24.620
He threads cable through an aluminum back
panel.

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.950
This cable will serve as a ground wire.

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:34.310
He pushes a pop rivet through a hole beside
the cable and slides the eyelid on the end of

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.600
the cable over it.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.150
He then pulls the pin through the rivet.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:44.030
He threads wires from the panel junction box
into the opening beside the ground cable.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.400
He tucks them into a flexible cable at the
back.

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:56.620
He rivets the aluminum backplate to the
solar panel array.

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:04.780
He pulls the wires from the junction box
through holes in a cover.

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:11.360
He sadr's electrical pins to the ends of the
wires and plugs them into a single connector.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.290
With a junction box cover installed.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:19.880
The solar street light system is ready for
final testing.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.380
The team assembles the streetlight to a pole
in the factory.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:29.640
They connect everything and stuff the wires
into the poles cavity.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:34.770
They screw the LED light fixture to the
bracket on the pole and it's ready to shine.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.830
The street lamp controller senses when it's
dark and automatically illuminates.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:48.130
It will also detect a well-lit environment
and switch the light off.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.340
Now that street smarts.

